  "title": "Is Your Non-Compete Worthless? Here's How to Fix It",
  "date": "2024-12-27T16:03:33.103Z",
  "content": {
    "text": "96\n\n Is Your Non-Compete Worthless? Here's How to Fix It\nHi Sara,\nLet\u2019s talk about non-competes\u2014a favorite topic for business owners, and one of the most misunderstood legal documents in existence.\u00a0\nI see it all the time: non-competes that sound airtight but, when tested, are about as useful as a screen door on a submarine.\nThe Big Mistake Everyone\u2019s Making\nThe question everyone asks is \u2018is this even enforceable\u2019? And the answer is usually at least partially yes. But the question people SHOULD be asking is \u2018does this even help me\u2019 and the answer to that is often \u2018nope, not a bit.\u2019\nMost non-competes include a geographical restriction. They say something like:\n\u201cYou can\u2019t compete within 20 miles of this business location.\u201d\nThat might have worked when businesses were local, and horse-drawn carriages were all the rage. But in 2024, when services are offered nationwide (hello, internet!), a 20-mile radius is laughably ineffective.\nWhy It Matters\nIf your clients are scattered across the country, what good is a non-compete that only covers a tiny local area? It\u2019s a loophole big enough to drive a semi-truck through. And trust me, if a former employee or buyer wants to compete with you, they will drive through it.\nHow to Fix It\nYour non-compete needs to reflect where your clients actually are. If your business operates nationwide, then your non-compete should restrict competitors from providing services to your client base, wherever they\u2019re located. Of course, you do have to balance that against federal and state laws relating to enforceability which is where all the nuance comes in and is why lawyers still have a job.\nDon\u2019t Let Your Non-Compete Work Against You\nNon-competes are meant to protect your business, not give you a false sense of security. If your current agreement isn\u2019t built for the way your business operates today, it\u2019s time for a rewrite.\nHave questions about making your non-compete actually enforceable? \nCheck out a recent episode of DealMakers Podcast where we discuss non-competes.\nTo stronger (and smarter) agreements,\nSara Sharp\nDeal Attorney, Partner\nSK&S Law Group\nBusiness Consultant and Founder of Deal Academy\n--\nFor more helpful lessons from Deal Academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down, where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out some recent Dealmakers podcast episodes by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2024\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
